x Let's talk about UI.
x 
x The first idea I want to share is that the UI will be designed for big clumsy
x fingers in turbulence. We're going to define a unit, the "thumb", that sizes buttons
x and widgets. Let's define it in one place so I can later say "make thumb 20% bigger"
x and all the widgets get bigger or "make thumb 20% smaller" and now there's more
x screen real estate under the widgets.
x 
x Let's begin with three main "pages" of the app.
x Chart page is tiled charts. Put a one-thumb button in the upper-left corner (with a 0.1 thumb margin
x so we can see the chart poking beyond) that shows the current chart. Clicking it will slide out a
x tray of one-thumb buttons to select among SECTIONAL, TAC, IFR-LOW, IFR-HIGH.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

x Trays are modal. They gray everything beyond the tray; clicking outside the tray closes it
x (without effecting a change).

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

At the bottom of the chart, centered, is a Nav Element. It's 2/3thumb high and 3 thumb wide.
It's split into two rows. The top row summarizes the current leg ("UBG -> OLM crs 342"),
the bottom row is a CDI depiction ("°  ° ^|  °  °") -- the triangle depicts current position,
in this case a little left of course. A "dot" deviation has a standard definition depending
on the flight plan leg (enroute vs approach).

The next page is the Flight Plan page. We navigate there by tapping the Nav Element.
This page is a table. On the left are waypoints (1 thumb high by 2.5 thumb wide). To the
right are columns ("distance (nm)", "ETE (h:m)", "course (°)", ... eventually configurable?).
Waypoints may be grouped into procedures, in which case the top waypoint in the list is the
procedure name ("RNTN3 departure"), and the waypoints that belong to the procedure are indented
0.1 thumb. (Keep this in mind, but we can start with simple flight plans that don't have
grouped procedures.)

Clicking on a waypoint brings up a modal (that covers the remaining columns in the table) with
actions associated with the waypoint: "remove", "insert", "reorder", "waypoint info", "Add airway",
"Select procedure", "Charts". Clicking "charts" navigates to the Chart top-level page.
(don't worry implementing the other actions yet.)

The third page is the Chart page. It displays plates and CSups. These are just flat PNGs,
not tiled layers. From a UI perspective, they act like the chart page: I can pinch/zoom and
drag-pan to be able to read small text. Dragging is allowed to pull the image one thumb
farther onto the screen than the edge of the display, so that we can read features at the
edge of the image out from under any UI elements we stuck over the display.
The Chart page has two one-thumb button in the upper-left corner. The left button shows
which airport we're looking at charts for ("KRNT"); clicking it pulls up a tray of
several airports that have been recently accessed on the chart page (or also appear
on the flight plan). The second button displays which chart we're viewing ("RNAV 16 Z", "CSup", "RNTN3").
Clicking it slides out a tray to select among the available charts for the currently selected airport.


