Database plotting

x Okay, let's talk about preparing to draw points (airports, intersections) and nonpoint
x features (airspace boundaries, TFRs) on the chart in the UI. You're not gonna do the UI
x work, but you need to prepare the preprocessing to make the UI run fast.
x The big concern here is that we don't want to have the UI need to iterate over the
x entire database.
x 
x Points seem easy: Put them in tiles at a single zoom level where the points-per-tile is
x neither too big nor too small. The app will iterate over all tiles that intersect the viewport,
x unless it's zoomed out far enough that the list is too big, in which case we won't draw that
x layer anyway.
x 
x What about non-point features, like airspace boundaries? They should also get segmented
x spatially, but cutting lines and curves at tile boundaries sucks. Maybe instead we have a
x list of bounding boxes and pointers into a table of boundary shapes. Then we tile the
x (bounding box,ptr) records, and put the records into every tile they overlap. We'll choose
x a zoom level where we're not ridiculously duplicating them. The UI will enumerate the tiles,
x gather all the bboxes that intersect its viewport, discarding duplicates (by some identifier),
x and that's the list of features to draw.
x 
x Anything else we need to think about to prepare vector data for realtime plotting in the UI?

x Preproc: We discovered on the UI side that the "tiled" vector (point) data is one big-ass json
x file. That ... kind of defeats the purpose of tiles. What I expected was a zip file with files
x named in quadtrees just like the tiled charts, but instead of a PNG, each file in a json with
x the records for just that region.

  On FAA boundary sources: I found official FAA indications that boundary-ish data is available as services, but not yet a clean
  static polygon product I’d build against.

